Summary: winemac: ActivationPolicy option; "accessory" gives no Dock icon and never activates
Category: input
Kind: feature
Feature: embedding
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Note: after the behaviour of CrossOver's CW HACK 24141, generalised to an
    option; none of that hack's code is used
Upstream: no
Why: A program run as a helper of an app that embeds Wine shouldn't put a
 Dock icon or a menu bar on screen, or take focus from the app. The Mac
 Driver "ActivationPolicy" value, read the usual two ways
 (HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver, overridden by AppDefaults\<exe>\Mac
 Driver), takes "regular" (the default, Wine's behaviour) or "accessory"
 (a string, either case; any other value means regular).
 The environment variable WINEMAC_ACTIVATION_POLICY, set to either,
 overrides the registry, so a launcher can set it for the programs it runs
 (installers during a build, say) without changing the prefix. With
 "accessory", topmost (WS_EX_TOPMOST) windows also stay at the normal
 window level, since the process is never active: TopmostFloatInactive is
 treated as none whatever it's set to, and -adjustWindowLevels doesn't
 raise them to its floor for floating windows (above the Dock, level 21),
 which otherwise applies whatever TopmostFloatInactive says.
 macOS also shows an accessory app's windows over another app's fullscreen
 Space, even while the accessory app is inactive; a regular app's windows
 aren't. Of the collection behaviours tried on a native accessory window
 (default, FullScreenPrimary, Managed, MoveToActiveSpace, Transient), only
 FullScreenNone kept it out. So with "accessory", -adjustFullScreenBehavior:
 clears FullScreenPrimary and FullScreenAuxiliary and sets FullScreenNone.
 With "accessory", the process gets NSApplicationActivationPolicyAccessory
 when it first shows a window, instead of the regular policy and the Wine
 menu, and the driver never asks macOS to activate it. Its windows are
 still shown. Any other value, or none, changes nothing.
Programs: Second Chance (an app that embeds Wine): wants its helper
 processes run without a Dock icon or menu bar and without taking focus
Seen on: macOS 26.7.2 (a native accessory app, and a Wine dialog in a
 Screen Slots session): an accessory app's windows show over another app's
 fullscreen Space; macOS 26.7 (Second Chance's installer wizards in front
 of a fullscreen game 3 times in 10 on 11.22-build15)
Enable: reg HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver\ActivationPolicy = accessory (REG_SZ)
 reg HKCU\Software\Wine\AppDefaults\<exe>\Mac Driver\ActivationPolicy = accessory (REG_SZ) (per program)
 env WINEMAC_ACTIVATION_POLICY=accessory (overrides the registry; inherited by child processes)
Known issues: macOS can still activate an accessory process when its
 window is clicked; the driver only stops activating it itself. With
 FullScreenNone, an accessory process's windows can't use macOS's own
 fullscreen (a Space of their own); Wine's usual fullscreen (a window
 covering the display) isn't affected.
Uncertain: not tested with a game run as accessory. In Second Chance's
 run nothing needed a click on a wizard kept off a game's Space, so
 reaching one that does wasn't tested. The regression test covers the
 activation policy only, not the topmost level or FullScreenNone.
Expects before it: winemac-cocoa-start-wait (context)
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-20: in 11.22-build0 (a local host build on meeka;
 suite 51 passed, 2 skipped, 0 failed). Second Chance's installer wizards,
 run as accessory beside fullscreen games, came in front of a game 0 times
 in 10, against 3 on 11.22-build15; every wizard still finished.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
@@ -223,6 +223,22 @@
 
     - (void) transformProcessToForeground:(BOOL)activateIfTransformed
     {
+        if (accessory_activation_policy)
+        {
+            /* Windows need at least the accessory policy to be shown; it
+               gives no Dock icon and no menu bar.  Never activate. */
+            if ([NSApp activationPolicy] != NSApplicationActivationPolicyAccessory)
+            {
+                [NSApp setActivationPolicy:NSApplicationActivationPolicyAccessory];
+                if (!enable_app_nap)
+                {
+                    [[[NSProcessInfo processInfo] beginActivityWithOptions:NSActivityUserInitiatedAllowingIdleSystemSleep
+                                                                    reason:@"Running Windows program"] retain]; // intentional leak
+                }
+            }
+            return;
+        }
+
         if ([NSApp activationPolicy] != NSApplicationActivationPolicyRegular)
         {
             NSMenu* mainMenu;
@@ -618,7 +634,10 @@
             NSInteger origLevel = [window level];
             NSInteger newLevel = [window minimumLevelForActive:active];
 
-            if (window.floating)
+            /* An accessory process is never active: its topmost windows keep
+               the level -minimumLevelForActive: gives them, instead of being
+               raised above the Dock, so they don't cover the active app. */
+            if (window.floating && !(accessory_activation_policy && newLevel < NSFloatingWindowLevel))
             {
                 if (minFloatingLevel <= maxNonfloatingLevel)
                     minFloatingLevel = maxNonfloatingLevel + 1;
@@ -1998,6 +2017,7 @@
         NSDictionary *userInfo;
 
         if ([NSApp isActive]) return;  /* Nothing to do. */
+        if (accessory_activation_policy) return;  /* Never activate. */
 
         if (!ignore ||
             ![NSApplication instancesRespondToSelector:@selector(yieldActivationToApplication:)])
diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
@@ -1044,6 +1044,16 @@
             behavior |= NSWindowCollectionBehaviorFullScreenAuxiliary;
             if (style & NSWindowStyleMaskFullScreen)
                 [super toggleFullScreen:nil];
+        }
+
+        /* macOS shows an accessory app's windows over another app's
+           fullscreen Space, even while the accessory app is inactive.
+           FullScreenNone keeps them out of it (and they can't go fullscreen
+           themselves, which a helper process doesn't need). */
+        if (accessory_activation_policy)
+        {
+            behavior &= ~(NSWindowCollectionBehaviorFullScreenPrimary | NSWindowCollectionBehaviorFullScreenAuxiliary);
+            behavior |= NSWindowCollectionBehaviorFullScreenNone;
         }
 
         if (behavior != [self collectionBehavior])
diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h
@@ -118,6 +118,7 @@
 extern bool retina_enabled;  /* Whether Retina mode is enabled via registry setting. */
 extern bool retina_on;       /* Whether Retina mode is currently active (enabled and display is in default mode). */
 extern bool enable_app_nap;
+extern bool accessory_activation_policy;
 extern int cocoa_start_timeout;
 
 static inline CGRect cgrect_mac_from_win(CGRect rect)
diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c
@@ -54,6 +54,7 @@
 int gl_surface_mode = GL_SURFACE_IN_FRONT_OPAQUE;
 bool retina_enabled = false;
 bool enable_app_nap = false;
+bool accessory_activation_policy = false;
 int cocoa_start_timeout = 60;
 
 pthread_key_t macdrv_thread_data_key = 0;
@@ -367,7 +368,30 @@
 
     if (!get_config_key(hkey, appkey, "EnableAppNap", buffer, sizeof(buffer)))
         enable_app_nap = IS_OPTION_TRUE(buffer[0]);
+
+    /* "accessory": no Dock icon or menu bar, and the driver never activates
+       the process; its windows are still shown.  For helper processes. */
+    if (!get_config_key(hkey, appkey, "ActivationPolicy", buffer, sizeof(buffer)))
+    {
+        static const WCHAR accessoryW[] = {'a','c','c','e','s','s','o','r','y',0};
+        accessory_activation_policy = !wcsicmp(buffer, accessoryW);
+    }
+
+    /* WINEMAC_ACTIVATION_POLICY ("accessory" or "regular") overrides the
+       registry: a launcher can set it for the programs it runs, and their
+       children inherit it. */
+    {
+        const char *env = getenv("WINEMAC_ACTIVATION_POLICY");
+        if (env && !strcasecmp(env, "accessory")) accessory_activation_policy = true;
+        else if (env && !strcasecmp(env, "regular")) accessory_activation_policy = false;
+    }
 
+    /* An accessory process is never active, and a topmost window of an
+       inactive process still floats (TopmostFloatInactive): keep them at the
+       normal level, so they don't go over the app that is active. */
+    if (accessory_activation_policy)
+        topmost_float_inactive = TOPMOST_FLOAT_INACTIVE_NONE;
+
     /* Seconds to wait for the main thread to start Cocoa.  Upstream waits 5,
        which a Mac with every core busy, under Rosetta, can miss. */
     if (!get_config_key(hkey, appkey, "CocoaStartTimeout", buffer, sizeof(buffer)))
